---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { absoluteUrl, site } from '../lib/site';

const aboutSchema = [
  {
    '@type': 'ProfilePage',
    '@id': absoluteUrl('/about'),
    url: absoluteUrl('/about'),
    name: `About ${site.author.name}`,
    description: site.author.bio,
    inLanguage: 'en',
    mainEntity: { '@id': `${site.url}/about#person` },
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About — A Working Engineer"
  description="About Jin Lo — a software engineer writing about architecture, performance, and the craft of building systems that last."
  path="/about"
  schema={aboutSchema}
>
  <div class="container">
    <header class="page-head">
      <div class="eyebrow mono">About</div>
      <h1>A working engineer, writing things down.</h1>
      <p class="lede">No personal brand, no growth funnel. Just the notes I wish I had found earlier.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="about-grid">
      <aside class="about-aside">
        <div class="avatar-plate" aria-hidden="true"><span>JL</span></div>
        <h2>{site.author.name}</h2>
        <p>{site.author.role}<br />{site.author.location}</p>
        <div class="aside-links">
          <a href={`mailto:${site.author.email}`}>Email</a>
          <a href="/rss.xml">RSS feed</a>
          {site.author.github && <a href={site.author.github} rel="me noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a>}
          {site.author.x && <a href={site.author.x} rel="me noopener noreferrer">X / Twitter</a>}
          {site.author.linkedin && <a href={site.author.linkedin} rel="me noopener noreferrer">LinkedIn</a>}
        </div>
      </aside>

      <div class="about-body">
        <h2>What I do</h2>
        <p>
          I build and maintain backend systems: APIs, data pipelines, and the unglamorous
          glue that keeps a product running on a Tuesday. Most of my work happens at the
          boundary between “this is elegant” and “this can be operated by three people at
          2 a.m.”
        </p>
        <p>
          I have spent the last decade shipping software in teams of four to forty, across
          time zones and time-budget constraints. Along the way I developed a strong
          preference for boring technology, explicit trade-offs, and documentation that
          respects the person who reads it six months later.
        </p>
        <p>
          I write in English for readers everywhere. The perspective is deliberately
          non-Silicon-Valley: fewer scale fantasies, more constraints, budgets, and the
          realities of maintaining what you built.
        </p>

        <h2>What this site is</h2>
        <p>
          A small, deliberate publication. Every essay starts from something concrete I
          worked on, measured, or got wrong. If a post has no first-hand artifact in it, it
          does not get published here.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is also an experiment in ownership: no trackers, no cookie wall, no algorithmic
          feed. Full text through RSS, email if you want it, and a plain website that rewards
          reading rather than scrolling.
        </p>

        <h2>Editorial standards</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Every essay starts from a concrete artifact: a system I worked on, a measurement I took, or a mistake I made.</li>
          <li>Claims that depend on external behavior link to official documentation or primary research.</li>
          <li>Code samples are meant to be run; when a sample is illustrative rather than executable, the text says so.</li>
          <li>Substantive corrections are made in place and reflected in the essay's updated date.</li>
          <li>No sponsors, affiliate links, or paid placement. The arguments are mine.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What I write about</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/architecture">Architecture</a> — boundaries, migrations, and long-lived decisions.</li>
          <li><a href="/performance">Performance</a> — what readers actually experience, measured.</li>
          <li><a href="/tags/engineering">Engineering</a> — types, tests, and tools that pay rent.</li>
          <li><a href="/tags/devops">DevOps</a> — pipelines and operations without heroics.</li>
          <li><a href="/tags/career">Career</a> — focus, feedback, and working across cultures.</li>
          <li><a href="/tags/writing">Writing</a> — clarity as an engineering skill.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Elsewhere</h2>
        <p>
          The best way to reach me is email: <a href={`mailto:${site.author.email}`}>{site.author.email}</a>.
          I read everything and reply to most of it, eventually. Found an error? Say so;
          substantive corrections are noted in the essay. If you want new essays without
          an algorithm in the middle, use <a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a> or
          <a href="/newsletter">the letter</a>.
        </p>

        <p class="colophon">
          <strong>Colophon.</strong> Set in Instrument Serif and Newsreader, with JetBrains
          Mono for metadata. Built with Astro, statically rendered, no client framework, no
          analytics by default. Hosted on a CDN. The source is plain Markdown in a git
          repository — the most durable CMS I know.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
